---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, SOCIAL } from '../consts';

const FACTS = [
	['Based in', 'Stuttgart area, Germany'],
	['Education', 'MSc Communication Engineering, TU Munich'],
	['Previously', 'Bosch, Mercedes-Benz Tech Motion'],
	['Now', 'Building Anovi, writing, and taking on selected work'],
	['Core stack', 'Python, TypeScript, React Native, FastAPI, PostgreSQL'],
	[
		'Focus',
		'Production AI · Mobile engineering · Full-stack product development · Technical writing',
	],
];
---

<Base
	title="About"
	description="AI/ML engineer in Germany. Machine learning in automotive and industrial systems, then a nutrition app shipped solo."
>
	<h1>About</h1>

	<div class="prose">
		<p>
			I am an AI/ML engineer. Before this I built machine learning systems in automotive and
			industrial settings: computer vision, gaze and pose models, explainable AI, transformer and
			speech systems, and the unglamorous data work underneath all of it. I studied Communication
			Engineering at TU Munich and wrote my thesis on AI applied to radar.
		</p>
		<p>
			Then I started building Anovi, a nutrition and meal planning app, on my own. Not because of a
			health scare, but because of a gap I kept running into: nutrition advice is usually accurate
			and rarely usable. It ignores what is in your kitchen, what is expiring, what you have time to
			cook, and what you actually like. The interesting problem was never the model. It was
			translating evidence into a decision someone can make on a Tuesday evening.
		</p>
		<p>
			Building it alone meant doing all of it. The schema, the recommendation logic, the LLM
			pipeline, the payments, the German localisation, the privacy impact assessment, the App Store
			submission. That range is the reason this site exists. I learned things that are worth writing
			down, and most of them are not the things people put in conference talks.
		</p>
		<p>
			What I write here follows one rule: every claim has a receipt. Where there is an upstream
			issue, the number is in the article. Where there isn't, the article shows the reproduction,
			the measurement, or the control that made me confident. That includes the work I reverted and
			the detectors that gave me false positives.
		</p>
	</div>

	<dl class="facts">
		{
			FACTS.map(([label, value]) => (
				<div>
					<dt>{label}</dt>
					<dd>{value}</dd>
				</div>
			))
		}
	</dl>

	<p class="links">
		<a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>Email</a>
		<a href={SOCIAL.github} rel="me noopener">GitHub</a>
		<a href={SOCIAL.linkedin} rel="me noopener">LinkedIn</a>
	</p>
</Base>

<style>
	.facts {
		margin: 2.5rem 0;
		border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline);
	}

	.facts div {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		gap: 0.25rem 1rem;
		padding-block: 0.6rem;
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline);
	}

	dt {
		flex: 0 0 9rem;
		color: var(--muted);
		font-size: 0.9375rem;
	}

	dd {
		margin: 0;
		flex: 1 1 16rem;
	}

	.links {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		gap: 1.5rem;
		font-size: 0.9375rem;
	}
</style>
